  "cached_tex": "\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{amsthm}\n\n\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\n\\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\section*{Problem Statement}\n\n\\subsection*{Setup}\n\nLet $X$ be a finite set and $\\sigma$ a permutation of $X$. The signature $\\mathrm{sgn}(\\sigma) \\in \\{\\pm 1\\}$ is defined as usual.\n\nLet $H = \\langle g \\rangle$ be a cyclic group. Let $J$ be a finite group of odd order equipped with an action of $H$ by automorphisms (i.e., $\\sigma_g \\in \\mathrm{Aut}(J)$). Let $U \\trianglelefteq J$ be a normal subgroup that is stable under the $H$-action.\n\nShow that\n\\[\n\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_J) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U}) \\cdot \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U),\n\\]\nwhere $g|_J$, $g|_U$, and $g|_{J/U}$ denote the permutations induced by $g$ on the underlying sets of $J$, $U$, and the coset space $J/U$, respectively.\n\n\\section*{Solution}\n\n\\subsection*{1. Summary}\n\n\\subsubsection*{a. Verdict}\n\nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is that the identity\n\\[\n\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_J) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U}) \\cdot \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U)\n\\]\nholds true under the given conditions.\n\n\\subsubsection*{b. Method Sketch}\n\nThe solution proceeds by analyzing the permutation representation of the automorphism induced by $g$.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Permutation Representations:} The signature of a permutation on a finite set $X$ is the determinant of the corresponding permutation operator on the vector space $\\mathbb{C}[X]$. We let $P_J$, $P_U$, and $P_{J/U}$ be the permutation operators on $\\mathbb{C}[J]$, $\\mathbb{C}[U]$, and $\\mathbb{C}[J/U]$ corresponding to the permutations $g|_J$, $g|_U$, and $g|_{J/U}$, respectively. The problem is equivalent to proving $\\det(P_J) = \\det(P_{J/U}) \\det(P_U)$.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Coset Decomposition and Vector Space Structure:} We choose a basis for $\\mathbb{C}[J]$ that is adapted to the coset decomposition of $J$ with respect to $U$. Let $T$ be a set of left coset representatives for $U$ in $J$. The vector space $\\mathbb{C}[J]$ decomposes as a direct sum $\\mathbb{C}[J] = \\bigoplus_{t \\in T} W_t$, where $W_t = \\mathrm{span}\\{e_{tu} \\mid u \\in U\\}$. The operator $P_J$ permutes these subspaces. If $g$ maps the coset $tU$ to $t'U$, then $P_J$ maps the subspace $W_t$ to $W_{t'}$.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Determinant of Block-Permutation Matrices:} The matrix of $P_J$ in this basis has a block structure where non-zero blocks appear according to the permutation $g|_{J/U}$. We prove the following general proposition:\n    \\textbf{Proposition:} Let $V = \\bigoplus_{c \\in C} W_c$ be a vector space, where $C$ is a finite index set of size $k$, and each $W_c$ has dimension $m$. Let $L: V \\to V$ be a linear operator that maps each subspace $W_c$ into $W_{\\pi(c)}$ for some permutation $\\pi$ of $C$. For each $c \\in C$, let $A_c$ be the matrix of the map $L|_{W_c}: W_c \\to W_{\\pi(c)}$. Let $M$ be the matrix of $L$. Then $\\det(M) = (\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi))^m \\prod_{c \\in C} \\det(A_c)$.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Application and Analysis of Blocks:} Applying this proposition to $P_J$, we get $\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_J) = \\det(P_J) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U})^{|U|} \\prod_{t \\in T} \\det(A_t)$. The linear map corresponding to each non-zero block $A_t$ is shown to correspond to a permutation on $U$ of the form $u \\mapsto v_t \\cdot \\sigma_g(u)$ for some $v_t \\in U$. The determinant is $\\det(A_t) = \\mathrm{sgn}(L_{v_t}) \\cdot \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U)$, where $L_{v_t}$ is the left-translation permutation on $U$ by $v_t$.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Key Lemma on Left Translations:} The crucial step is proving the following lemma:\n    \\textbf{Lemma:} Let $G$ be a finite group of odd order. For any element $h \\in G$, the signature of the left-translation permutation $L_h: x \\mapsto hx$ is $\\mathrm{sgn}(L_h) = 1$.\n    Since $|J|$ is odd, its subgroup $U$ also has odd order. The lemma implies $\\mathrm{sgn}(L_{v_t}) = 1$ for all $t \\in T$.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Conclusion:} Using the lemma, we find $\\det(A_t) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U)$ for all $t \\in T$. Substituting this into the determinant formula gives $\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_J) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U})^{|U|} (\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U))^{|J/U|}$. Since $|J|$ is odd, the orders $|U|$ and $|J/U|$ are also odd. This allows us to remove the exponents, yielding the desired identity.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{2. Detailed Solution}\n\nLet $\\sigma_g \\in \\mathrm{Aut}(J)$ be the automorphism corresponding to the action of $g \\in H$. The permutations in question are $g|_J: j \\mapsto \\sigma_g(j)$ for $j \\in J$, $g|_U: u \\mapsto \\sigma_g(u)$ for $u \\in U$ (since $U$ is $H$-stable), and $g|_{J/U}: jU \\mapsto \\sigma_g(j)U$ for $jU \\in J/U$ (since $U$ is normal and $\\sigma_g$ is an automorphism).\n\nThe signature of a permutation $\\pi$ on a finite set $X$ is the determinant of the linear operator $P_\\pi$ on the complex vector space $\\mathbb{C}[X]$ with basis $\\{e_x\\}_{x \\in X}$, where $P_\\pi(e_x) = e_{\\pi(x)}$. Let $P_J$, $P_U$, and $P_{J/U}$ be the permutation operators on $\\mathbb{C}[J]$, $\\mathbb{C}[U]$, and $\\mathbb{C}[J/U]$ corresponding to $g|_J$, $g|_U$, and $g|_{J/U}$. The identity we want to prove is equivalent to\n\\[\n\\det(P_J) = \\det(P_{J/U}) \\cdot \\det(P_U).\n\\]\n\nFirst, we establish a key lemma.\n\n\\begin{lemma}\nLet $G$ be a finite group of odd order. For any element $h \\in G$, the signature of the left-translation permutation $L_h: G \\to G$ defined by $L_h(x) = hx$ is $\\mathrm{sgn}(L_h) = 1$.\n\\end{lemma}\n\n\\begin{proof}\nThe permutation $L_h$ decomposes into disjoint cycles. The cycles are the right cosets of the cyclic subgroup $\\langle h \\rangle$ in $G$. Let $|h|$ be the order of $h$. There are $|G|/|h|$ such cosets, and each has size $|h|$. Thus, $L_h$ consists of $|G|/|h|$ cycles, each of length $|h|$. The signature of a permutation on a set of size $N$ with $c$ cycles is given by $(-1)^{N-c}$. For $L_h$, we have $N = |G|$ and $c = |G|/|h|$. So,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{sgn}(L_h) = (-1)^{|G| - |G|/|h|}.\n\\]\nBy hypothesis, $|G|$ is odd. Since $|h|$ must divide $|G|$, $|h|$ is also odd. Consequently, the quotient $|G|/|h|$ is also odd. Therefore, the exponent $|G| - |G|/|h|$ is a difference of two odd integers, which is an even integer. Thus, $\\mathrm{sgn}(L_h) = (-1)^{\\text{even}} = 1$. This completes the proof of the lemma.\n\\end{proof}\n\nNow, we return to the main proof. Let $T$ be a set of representatives for the left cosets of $U$ in $J$. Then every element $j \\in J$ has a unique representation $j = tu$ for some $t \\in T$ and $u \\in U$. Let $C=T$ be the index set for the cosets. Let $k = |T| = |J/U|$ and $m = |U|$. We choose a basis for $\\mathbb{C}[J]$ adapted to this decomposition. The vector space $\\mathbb{C}[J]$ decomposes as a direct sum $\\mathbb{C}[J] = \\bigoplus_{t \\in T} W_t$, where $W_t = \\mathrm{span}\\{e_{tu} \\mid u \\in U\\}$.\n\nThe operator $P_J$ acts on a basis vector $e_{tu}$ as follows:\n\\[\nP_J(e_{tu}) = e_{\\sigma_g(tu)} = e_{\\sigma_g(t)\\sigma_g(u)}.\n\\]\nThe permutation $g|_{J/U}$ acts on the set of cosets $\\{tU \\mid t \\in T\\}$. Let $\\pi$ be the permutation on $T$ such that $\\sigma_g(t)U = \\pi(t)U$. This means that for each $t \\in T$, there exists a unique $v_t \\in U$ such that $\\sigma_g(t) = \\pi(t)v_t$. The action of $P_J$ on a basis vector of $W_t$ is\n\\[\nP_J(e_{tu}) = e_{\\pi(t)v_t\\sigma_g(u)}.\n\\]\nSince $v_t\\sigma_g(u) \\in U$, the vector $e_{\\pi(t)v_t\\sigma_g(u)}$ is in $W_{\\pi(t)}$. This shows that $P_J$ maps the subspace $W_t$ to the subspace $W_{\\pi(t)}$.\n\nLet $M$ be the matrix of $P_J$ with respect to the basis ordered by cosets. $M$ has a block structure determined by $\\pi$. We now prove a general proposition for the determinant of such a matrix.\n\n\\begin{proposition}\nLet $V = \\bigoplus_{c \\in C} W_c$ be a vector space, where $C$ is a finite index set of size $k$, and each $W_c$ has dimension $m$. Let $L: V \\to V$ be a linear operator that maps each subspace $W_c$ into $W_{\\pi(c)}$ for some permutation $\\pi$ of $C$. For each $c \\in C$, let $A_c$ be the matrix of the map $L|_{W_c}: W_c \\to W_{\\pi(c)}$ with respect to some chosen bases. Let $M$ be the matrix of $L$ with respect to the combined basis for $V$. Then $\\det(M) = (\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi))^m \\prod_{c \\in C} \\det(A_c)$.\n\\end{proposition}\n\n\\begin{proof}\nLet $M$ be the matrix of $L$ arranged as a $k \\times k$ block matrix, where the blocks $M_{rc}$ are $m \\times m$ matrices representing the map from $W_c$ to $W_r$. The condition $L(W_c) \\subseteq W_{\\pi(c)}$ implies that $M_{rc} = 0$ for $r \\neq \\pi(c)$. The matrix $A_c$ is the block $M_{\\pi(c), c}$. Thus, $M$ has exactly one non-zero block in each block-column $c$ (namely $A_c$ in block-row $\\pi(c)$) and in each block-row $r$ (namely $A_{\\pi^{-1}(r)}$ in block-column $\\pi^{-1}(r)$).\n\nLet $P_{\\pi^{-1}}$ be the $k \\times k$ block permutation matrix corresponding to $\\pi^{-1}$, where each block is an $m \\times m$ identity matrix $I_m$. The $(r,s)$-th block of $P_{\\pi^{-1}}$ is $\\delta_{r, \\pi^{-1}(s)}I_m$. The determinant of this matrix is $\\det(P_{\\pi^{-1}}) = (\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi^{-1}))^m = (\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi))^m$.\n\nConsider the matrix product $P_{\\pi^{-1}} M$. The $(r,c)$-th block of this product is given by\n\\[ (P_{\\pi^{-1}} M)_{rc} = \\sum_{s \\in C} (P_{\\pi^{-1}})_{rs} M_{sc} = \\sum_{s \\in C} \\delta_{r, \\pi^{-1}(s)}I_m M_{sc} = M_{\\pi(r), c}. \\]\nThe blocks of $M$ are $M_{sc} = \\delta_{s, \\pi(c)} A_c$. Substituting this, the $(r,c)$-th block of $P_{\\pi^{-1}} M$ is\n\\[ M_{\\pi(r), c} = \\delta_{\\pi(r), \\pi(c)} A_c. \\]\nSince $\\pi$ is a permutation, $\\pi(r) = \\pi(c)$ if and only if $r=c$. Thus, the block is $\\delta_{r,c} A_c$. This shows that $P_{\\pi^{-1}} M$ is a block-diagonal matrix:\n\\[ P_{\\pi^{-1}} M = \\mathrm{diag}(A_c)_{c \\in C}. \\]\nTaking the determinant of both sides, we have\n\\[ \\det(P_{\\pi^{-1}} M) = \\det(P_{\\pi^{-1}}) \\det(M) = (\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi))^m \\det(M). \\]\nThe determinant of the block-diagonal matrix is the product of the determinants of the diagonal blocks:\n\\[ \\det(\\mathrm{diag}(A_c)_{c \\in C}) = \\prod_{c \\in C} \\det(A_c). \\]\nEquating the two expressions gives\n\\[ (\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi))^m \\det(M) = \\prod_{c \\in C} \\det(A_c). \\]\nSince $(\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi))^m = \\pm 1$, its inverse is itself. Multiplying by $(\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi))^m$ yields\n\\[ \\det(M) = (\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi))^m \\prod_{c \\in C} \\det(A_c). \\]\nThis proves the proposition.\n\\end{proof}\n\nApplying the proposition to the operator $P_J$, we have $\\pi$ as the permutation on $T$ induced by $g|_{J/U}$, so $\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U})$. The block size is $m=|U|$. Thus,\n\\[\n\\det(P_J) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U})^{|U|} \\prod_{t \\in T} \\det(A_t).\n\\]\nNow we analyze the determinants of the blocks $A_t$. The matrix $A_t$ represents the map from $W_t$ to $W_{\\pi(t)}$ given by $e_{tu} \\mapsto e_{\\pi(t)v_t\\sigma_g(u)}$. We can identify $W_t$ with $\\mathbb{C}[U]$ via the isomorphism $\\phi_t(e_u) = e_{tu}$. The map represented by $A_t$ corresponds to the map $L_t = \\phi_{\\pi(t)}^{-1} \\circ P_J \\circ \\phi_t: \\mathbb{C}[U] \\to \\mathbb{C}[U]$. For any $u \\in U$,\n\\[\nL_t(e_u) = \\phi_{\\pi(t)}^{-1}(P_J(e_{tu})) = \\phi_{\\pi(t)}^{-1}(e_{\\pi(t)v_t\\sigma_g(u)}) = e_{v_t\\sigma_g(u)}.\n\\]\nThis shows that $L_t$ is the permutation operator on $\\mathbb{C}[U]$ corresponding to the permutation $\\tau_t: u \\mapsto v_t\\sigma_g(u)$ on $U$. This permutation is the composition of the automorphism $g|_U: u \\mapsto \\sigma_g(u)$ and the left translation $L_{v_t}: u \\mapsto v_t u$. So, $\\tau_t = L_{v_t} \\circ (g|_U)$. The determinant of its operator is the signature of the permutation:\n\\[\n\\det(A_t) = \\det(L_t) = \\mathrm{sgn}(\\tau_t) = \\mathrm{sgn}(L_{v_t} \\circ (g|_U)) = \\mathrm{sgn}(L_{v_t}) \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U).\n\\]\nThe group $J$ has odd order. Since $U$ is a subgroup of $J$, its order $|U|$ must divide $|J|$ by Lagrange's theorem. Any divisor of an odd number is odd, so $|U|$ is odd. By our lemma, the signature of the left translation by any element $v_t \\in U$ is 1. Thus, $\\mathrm{sgn}(L_{v_t}) = 1$.\nThis simplifies $\\det(A_t)$ to $\\det(A_t) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U)$.\n\nNow we can compute the product of the determinants of the blocks:\n\\[\n\\prod_{t \\in T} \\det(A_t) = \\prod_{t \\in T} \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U) = (\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U))^{|T|}.\n\\]\nHere $|T| = |J/U|$. Putting everything together:\n\\[\n\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_J) = \\det(P_J) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U})^{|U|} (\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U))^{|J/U|}.\n\\]\nSince $|J|$ is odd, both $|U|$ and $|J/U| = |J|/|U|$ are odd integers. For any $s \\in \\{\\pm 1\\}$, if $n$ is an odd integer, then $s^n=s$. Therefore:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U})^{|U|} = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U})$ since $|U|$ is odd.\n    \\item $(\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U))^{|J/U|} = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U)$ since $|J/U|$ is odd.\n\\end{itemize}\nSubstituting these into our equation gives:\n\\[\n\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_J) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U}) \\cdot \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U).\n\\]\nThis completes the proof.\n\n\\section*{Verification Report}\n\n\\subsection*{Summary}\n\n\\textbf{Final Verdict:} The solution is correct.\n\n\\textbf{List of Findings:}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item There are no issues in the provided solution.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{Detailed Verification Log}\n\n\\textbf{Step 1: Rephrasing the problem in terms of linear algebra}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"The signature of a permutation $\\pi$ on a finite set $X$ is the determinant of the linear operator $P_\\pi$ on the complex vector space $\\mathbb{C}[X]$... The identity we want to prove is equivalent to $\\det(P_J) = \\det(P_{J/U}) \\cdot \\det(P_U)$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Analysis:} This is a standard and correct way to work with signatures. The definitions of the permutations $g|_J$, $g|_U$, and $g|_{J/U}$ are accurate. The permutation $g|_{J/U}$ is well-defined because $U$ is a normal subgroup and is stable under the automorphism $\\sigma_g$. This step is correct.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Step 2: Lemma on the signature of left-translation}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"\\textbf{Lemma:} Let $G$ be a finite group of odd order. For any element $h \\in G$, the signature of the left-translation permutation $L_h: G \\to G$ defined by $L_h(x) = hx$ is $\\mathrm{sgn}(L_h) = 1$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Analysis:} The proof of the lemma is sound.\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item The decomposition of the permutation $L_h$ into disjoint cycles corresponding to the right cosets of $\\langle h \\rangle$ is correct.\n        \\item The number of cycles is correctly identified as $c = |G|/|h|$, and the length of each cycle is $|h|$.\n        \\item The formula for the signature, $\\mathrm{sgn}(L_h) = (-1)^{|G|-c} = (-1)^{|G| - |G|/|h|}$, is correct.\n        \\item The argument that since $|G|$ is odd, its divisor $|h|$ must also be odd, and consequently the quotient $|G|/|h|$ is odd, is correct.\n        \\item The exponent $|G| - |G|/|h|$ is the difference of two odd integers, which is an even integer. Thus, $\\mathrm{sgn}(L_h) = 1$.\n        \\item The lemma and its proof are correct.\n    \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Step 3: Decomposition of the vector space}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"Let $T$ be a set of representatives for the left cosets of $U$ in $J$. Then every element $j \\in J$ has a unique representation $j = tu$ for some $t \\in T$ and $u \\in U$. ... The vector space $\\mathbb{C}[J]$ decomposes as a direct sum $\\mathbb{C}[J] = \\bigoplus_{t \\in T} W_t$, where $W_t = \\mathrm{span}\\{e_{tu} \\mid u \\in U\\}$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Analysis:} This is a correct decomposition of the vector space $\\mathbb{C}[J]$ based on the partition of the set $J$ by the left cosets of $U$. This step is correct.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Step 4: Analysis of the operator's block structure}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"The operator $P_J$ acts on a basis vector $e_{tu}$ as follows: $P_J(e_{tu}) = e_{\\sigma_g(tu)} = e_{\\sigma_g(t)\\sigma_g(u)}$. ... for each $t \\in T$, there exists a unique $v_t \\in U$ such that $\\sigma_g(t) = \\pi(t)v_t$. ... This shows that $P_J$ maps the subspace $W_t$ to the subspace $W_{\\pi(t)}$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Analysis:} The derivation is correct. The permutation $\\pi$ on the set of representatives $T$ is well-defined. The existence and uniqueness of $v_t \\in U$ for each $t \\in T$ is correctly established. The conclusion that $P_J(W_t) \\subseteq W_{\\pi(t)}$ is a key and correct observation that establishes the block structure of the matrix representation of $P_J$. This step is correct.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Step 5: Proposition on the determinant of a block matrix}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"\\textbf{Proposition:} Let $V = \\bigoplus_{c \\in C} W_c$... Then $\\det(M) = (\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi))^m \\prod_{c \\in C} \\det(A_c)$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Analysis:} The proposition is stated and proven with full rigor. The proof correctly constructs a block permutation matrix $P_{\\pi^{-1}}$ to transform the matrix $M$ into a block-diagonal matrix. The determinant of $P_{\\pi^{-1}}$ is correctly calculated as $(\\mathrm{sgn}(\\pi))^m$. The argument using $\\det(P_{\\pi^{-1}}M) = \\det(P_{\\pi^{-1}})\\det(M)$ is sound and leads to the stated result. This step is correct.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Step 6: Application of the proposition}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"Applying the proposition to the operator $P_J$, we have ... $\\det(P_J) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U})^{|U|} \\prod_{t \\in T} \\det(A_t)$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Analysis:} This is a direct and correct application of the previously proven proposition. The permutation $\\pi$ corresponds to $g|_{J/U}$, the index set size is $|T|=|J/U|$, and the block dimension is $m=|U|$. This step is correct.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Step 7: Analysis of the block determinants $\\det(A_t)$}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"The map represented by $A_t$ corresponds to the map $L_t ... L_t(e_u) = e_{v_t\\sigma_g(u)}$. ... $\\det(A_t) = \\mathrm{sgn}(L_{v_t}) \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U)$. ... By our lemma, ... $\\mathrm{sgn}(L_{v_t}) = 1$. This simplifies $\\det(A_t)$ to $\\det(A_t) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U)$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Analysis:} The analysis of the block matrices $A_t$ is excellent. The solution correctly identifies the permutation on $U$ corresponding to the action within each block. This permutation is correctly identified as a composition of the automorphism $g|_U$ and a left translation $L_{v_t}$. The signature is multiplicative, so $\\mathrm{sgn}(\\tau_t) = \\mathrm{sgn}(L_{v_t})\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U)$. The argument that $|U|$ is odd (since it divides $|J|$) is correct, which justifies the use of the lemma to conclude $\\mathrm{sgn}(L_{v_t})=1$. The final conclusion for this step, $\\det(A_t) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U)$, is correct.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Step 8: Final calculation}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"$\\prod_{t \\in T} \\det(A_t) = (\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U))^{|T|} = (\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U))^{|J/U|}$. Putting everything together: $\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_J) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U})^{|U|} (\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U))^{|J/U|}$. Since $|J|$ is odd, both $|U|$ and $|J/U|$ are odd integers. ... $s^n=s$ [for $s=\\pm 1$, $n$ odd]. ... $\\mathrm{sgn}(g|_J) = \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_{J/U}) \\cdot \\mathrm{sgn}(g|_U)$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Analysis:} The final steps of the calculation are correct. The product of the block determinants is correctly computed. This is substituted back into the main formula. The property that raising $\\pm 1$ to an odd power does not change its value is correctly invoked for the odd integers $|U|$ and $|J/U|$, leading to the final desired identity. This step is correct.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\end{document}"
